(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["mono",{"l:mono/next-greater-element-the-monotonic-stack-and-its":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan left to right with a stack of indices still waiting for their next greater element. When a value x arrives, every waiting element smaller than x has just found its answer: pop it and record x. Then push x. The waiting values never increase from bottom to top, which is what makes the stack \u003cb>monotonic\u003c/b>, and each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the whole pass is \u003cb>O(n)\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant.\u003c/b> After processing a[0..i], the stack holds exactly the indices j ≤ i with no greater element in (j, i], in index order, and their values are non-increasing from bottom to top. (If j &lt; j' were both on the stack with a[j] &lt; a[j'], then a[j'] would be a greater element after j, and j would already have been popped.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why the pop is correct.\u003c/b> When x = a[i] pops j, x &gt; a[j], and nothing between j and i was greater than a[j], or it would have popped j earlier. So x is the \u003ci>first\u003c/i> greater element. Stopping at the first top that is at least x is safe, because everything below it is at least as large. Indices still on the stack at the end have no greater element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why it is O(n).\u003c/b> The inner \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> looks quadratic, so count pops instead of iterations. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so all the inner loops together pop at most n times. Total work: n pushes, at most n pops, and n comparisons that end a while loop, at most 3n steps. With a potential function, let Φ be the stack size: a step with p pops costs 1 + p and changes Φ by 1 - p, so cost plus change in Φ is always 2. A single step can still pop O(n): on 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 6 the last value pops five.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Variants\u003c/b> (flip every comparison for smaller):\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Pop while\u003c/th>\u003cth>Answer is\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>next greater\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[top] &lt; x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>x, for each popped index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>next greater or equal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[top] &lt;= x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>x, for each popped index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>previous greater\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[top] &lt;= x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the top after popping, or -1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>previous greater or equal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[top] &lt; x\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the top after popping, or -1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shapes of the same pass.\u003c/b> Distances instead of values (days until warmer): record \u003ccode>i - j\u003c/code> at pop time. Circular arrays: loop i over 0 to 2n - 1 using \u003ccode>a[i % n]\u003c/code> and push only during the first lap. Online spans: store \u003ccode>(price, span)\u003c/code> pairs and add each popped span to today's, because the days a popped price covered are covered by today too.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def next_greater(a):\n    \"\"\"ans[i] = first value to the right of i that is greater than a[i], or -1.\"\"\"\n    ans = [-1] * len(a)\n    stack = []                          # indices still waiting; their values never increase upwards\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        while stack and a[stack[-1]] &lt; x:\n            ans[stack.pop()] = x        # x is the first greater value for that index\n        stack.append(i)\n    return ans\n\n\ndef prev_greater(a):\n    \"\"\"Index of the nearest element to the left that is &gt; a[i], or -1.\"\"\"\n    res, stack = [-1] * len(a), []\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        while stack and a[stack[-1]] &lt;= x:\n            stack.pop()                 # can never be anyone's previous greater again\n        res[i] = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n        stack.append(i)\n    return res\n\n\nclass StockSpanner:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []                 # (price, span); prices strictly decrease upwards\n\n    def next(self, price):\n        span = 1\n        while self.stack and self.stack[-1][0] &lt;= price:\n            span += self.stack.pop()[1]  # absorb the days that price covered\n        self.stack.append((price, span))\n        return span\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0, x = 5: the stack is empty, nothing to resolve; push index 0 (shown as index:value)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index    0   1   2   3   4   5\na        5   3   1   4   2   6\nans     -1  -1  -1  -1  -1  -1\nstack   [\u003cmark>0:5\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, x = 3: the top 5 is not smaller, so nothing is resolved; push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans     -1  -1  -1  -1  -1  -1\nstack   [0:5, \u003cmark>1:3\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2, x = 1: push. Values 5, 3, 1 never increase upwards: the invariant\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans     -1  -1  -1  -1  -1  -1\nstack   [0:5, 1:3, \u003cmark>2:1\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3, x = 4: 1 &lt; 4, pop index 2 and record 4; 3 &lt; 4, pop index 1 and record 4; 5 is not smaller, stop; push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans     -1  \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  -1  -1  -1\nstack   [0:5, \u003cmark>3:4\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, x = 2: the top 4 is not smaller; push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans     -1   4   4  -1  -1  -1\nstack   [0:5, 3:4, \u003cmark>4:2\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5, x = 6: pops indices 4, 3 and 0 in turn, recording 6 for each; push 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans     \u003cmark> 6\u003c/mark>   4   4  \u003cmark> 6\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark> 6\u003c/mark>  -1\nstack   [\u003cmark>5:6\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">End: index 5 was never popped, so it has no greater element and keeps -1. Six pushes, five pops\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ans      6   4   4   6   6  -1\nstack   [5:6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Storing values instead of indices: you cannot fill answers by position or compute distances.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Strictness with duplicates: \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> decides whether an equal value counts. Test on \u003ccode>[2, 2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Circular arrays: push only in the first lap (pushing again in the second lap is wasted work), and never push \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> itself past n - 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Forgetting the default for indices never popped: -1 for values, 0 for distances.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run \u003ccode>next_greater\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>[2, 7, 3, 5, 4, 6, 8]\u003c/code>. Which indices does the value 6 pop, and what is the final answer list?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At 6 the stack holds indices 1, 3, 4 (values 7, 5, 4); 6 pops index 4 and then index 3, and stops at 7. The final answers are \u003ccode>[7, 8, 5, 6, 6, 8, -1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer nearest greater or smaller questions in one pass and prove the pass is O(n) by counting pops.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mono/largest-rectangle-in-a-histogram":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The best rectangle has some bar as its shortest bar, and with that bar fixed it spreads left and right until a lower bar stops it. Keep a stack of bars with increasing heights. When a lower bar arrives, the bar on top is popped, and at that moment both of its limits are known: the arriving bar on the right and the bar below it in the stack on the left. One pass, O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>From brute force.\u003c/b> For each bar, walk outwards to the nearest lower bar on each side: O(n^2). Those limits are exactly the previous smaller and next smaller elements, so two monotonic passes give O(n); the one-pass version reads both limits at pop time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant.\u003c/b> The stack holds indices with strictly increasing heights (an equal bar pops its twin). For the top bar t, the bar below it, \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, is the nearest bar to the left of t that is lower, and every bar strictly between them is at least as tall as t.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Width at pop time.\u003c/b> When bar i pops t, \u003ccode>heights[i] &lt;= heights[t]\u003c/code>, and every bar between t and i is at least \u003ccode>heights[t]\u003c/code> tall (a lower one would have popped t first). So a rectangle of height \u003ccode>heights[t]\u003c/code> fits across the open interval (left, i): width \u003ccode>i - left - 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicates.\u003c/b> With \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code>, an equal bar pops its twin, and the twin may record a width that is too short. That is harmless: take any optimal rectangle and its \u003ci>rightmost\u003c/i> shortest bar. That bar is popped by a strictly lower bar (or the sentinel), and its left limit is exact, so its full width is recorded. Popping only on \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> is also correct; then the leftmost shortest bar gets the full width.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sentinel.\u003c/b> Appending a height-0 bar pops everything at the end, so no second loop is needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>In 2D\u003c/b> (Maximal Rectangle): for each row, let \u003ccode>heights[c]\u003c/code> count the consecutive 1s ending at that row in column c; each row is then a histogram, so run the pass per row for O(rows × cols).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> Each index is pushed and popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space for the stack.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def largest_rectangle(heights):\n    stack = []                          # indices of bars with increasing heights\n    best = 0\n    for i, h in enumerate(heights + [0]):           # the 0 flushes every bar at the end\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] &gt;= h:\n            top = stack.pop()                       # i is the first bar right of top that is not taller\n            left = stack[-1] if stack else -1       # nearest bar left of top that is lower\n            best = max(best, heights[top] * (i - left - 1))\n        stack.append(i)\n    return best\n\n\ndef maximal_rectangle(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return 0\n    heights, best = [0] * len(grid[0]), 0\n    for row in grid:\n        heights = [h + 1 if c == \"1\" else 0 for h, c in zip(heights, row)]\n        best = max(best, largest_rectangle(heights))\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Heights 2, 4, 5, 3, 1, 3. The answer is 9: height 3 across bars 1 to 3. i = 0: push bar 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>            #\n        #   #\n        #   #   #       #\n    #   #   #   #       #\n    #   #   #   #   #   #\n    0   1   2   3   4   5\nstack [\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 2   best 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1 (h 4) and i = 2 (h 5): each is taller than the top, so both are pushed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [0, 1, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 2 4 5   best 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3 (h 3): pop bar 2 (h 5, left 1, width 3 - 1 - 1 = 1, area 5), then bar 1 (h 4, left 0, width 2, area 8); push 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [0, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 2 3   best \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4 (h 1): pop bar 3 (h 3, left 0, width 4 - 0 - 1 = 3, area 9), then bar 0 (h 2, left -1, width 4, area 8); push 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [\u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 1   best \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5 (h 3): taller than the top, push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [4, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 1 3   best 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 6 is the sentinel 0: pop bar 5 (h 3, left 4, width 1, area 3), then bar 4 (h 1, left -1, width 6, area 6). Answer 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [\u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]   heights on stack 0   best 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Width off by one: it is \u003ccode>i - left - 1\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>left = -1\u003c/code> when the stack is empty, not \u003ccode>i - top\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No final flush: without the sentinel, \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> never pops and returns 0 instead of 4.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Appending the sentinel to the caller's list mutates it; \u003ccode>heights + [0]\u003c/code> makes a copy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In other languages, \u003ccode>height * width\u003c/code> can overflow 32-bit integers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace, why is the area for bar 3 (height 3) computed at i = 4 and not earlier, and which limits does it get?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A bar stays on the stack until a bar no taller than it arrives, and bar 4 (height 1) is the first. At that moment the bar below it in the stack is bar 0 (height 2), so its rectangle spans bars 1 to 3: width 4 - 0 - 1 = 3, area 9.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find every bar's limits with one rising stack and turn them into the largest rectangle in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mono/contribution-counting-sum-of-subarray-minimums":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Instead of visiting all n(n + 1)/2 subarrays, turn the sum around: each element a[i] is the minimum of a whole block of subarrays, those that start after its previous smaller element and end before its next smaller element. Count that block with two monotonic passes, multiply by a[i], add up: O(n). With equal values, make one side strict and the other non-strict, so every subarray's minimum is credited to exactly one position.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The block.\u003c/b> Let L be the index of the previous element \u003ci>strictly\u003c/i> smaller than a[i] (or -1), and R the index of the next element \u003ci>smaller than or equal to\u003c/i> a[i] (or n). Every subarray that starts in (L, i] and ends in [i, R) has a[i] as its minimum, and a[i] is its \u003ci>rightmost\u003c/i> minimum: values between L and i are at least a[i], values between i and R are greater. There are \u003ccode>(i - L) * (R - i)\u003c/code> such subarrays (choices of start times choices of end).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exactly once.\u003c/b> Every subarray has exactly one rightmost minimum, so the blocks partition all subarrays: the counts add up to n(n + 1)/2. With both sides strict, a subarray containing two equal minima is credited to both: \u003ccode>[2, 2]\u003c/code> would give 8 instead of 6. With both non-strict, it is credited to neither.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two passes.\u003c/b> Previous strictly smaller: scan left to right, popping while \u003ccode>a[top] &gt;= a[i]\u003c/code>. Next smaller or equal: scan right to left, popping while \u003ccode>a[top] &gt; a[i]\u003c/code>. Each is the monotonic stack pass, so O(n) time and space in total.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One pass.\u003c/b> When index j is popped by i (with \u003ccode>a[i] &lt;= a[j]\u003c/code>), i is j's right limit and the new top is its left limit; add \u003ccode>a[j] * (j - left) * (i - j)\u003c/code> and flush with a minus-infinity sentinel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Relatives.\u003c/b> Flip the comparisons to sum maxima; sum of subarray ranges (in the problem bank) is the sum of maxima minus the sum of minima. \"Fix the element that is the minimum, find its block\" also drives problems that combine the minimum with a range sum (add prefix sums).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Modulo.\u003c/b> The answer to the practice problem is taken modulo 10^9 + 7. Python integers never overflow, so reduce at the end; in C++ or Java use 64-bit arithmetic and reduce every product.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def sum_subarray_mins(a):\n    n = len(a)\n    left, right = [0] * n, [0] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n):                  # previous strictly smaller\n        while stack and a[stack[-1]] &gt;= a[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        left[i] = i - (stack[-1] if stack else -1)\n        stack.append(i)\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):      # next smaller or equal\n        while stack and a[stack[-1]] &gt; a[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        right[i] = (stack[-1] if stack else n) - i\n        stack.append(i)\n    return sum(x * l * r for x, l, r in zip(a, left, right))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>a = [3, 1, 2, 1]\u003c/code> (ten subarrays):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>index                    0    1    2    3\na                        3    1    2    1\nL: prev strictly smaller -1   -1    1   -1\nR: next smaller or equal  1    3    3    4\ni - L                     1    2    1    4\nR - i                     1    2    1    1\nsubarrays credited        1    4    1    4    total 10 = 4 * 5 / 2\ncontribution              3    4    2    4    sum 13\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Index 1 is credited with \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[3, 1]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[1, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2]\u003c/code>: its block stops before index 3, the equal 1. The two subarrays containing both 1s, \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2, 1]\u003c/code>, go to index 3, their rightmost minimum. A brute force over all ten subarrays also gives 13.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Same strictness on both sides: double counting (both strict) or missing subarrays (both non-strict). Test on \u003ccode>[2, 2]\u003c/code> (answer 6) and \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1]\u003c/code> (answer 6).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Off by one: the count of starts is \u003ccode>i - L\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>i - L - 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Swapping which pass gets the strict comparison is fine, as long as the two passes differ.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For \u003ccode>a = [2, 2]\u003c/code>, compute L, R and the count for each index with the template's rules, and show that the total is 6.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Index 0: no previous strictly smaller (L = -1), next smaller or equal is index 1 (R = 1), so it gets 1 × 1 = 1 subarray, \u003ccode>[2]\u003c/code>. Index 1: L = -1, R = 2, so 2 × 1 = 2 subarrays, \u003ccode>[2, 2]\u003c/code> and the second \u003ccode>[2]\u003c/code>. Total 2 × (1 + 2) = 6, which matches 2 + 2 + 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to sum a minimum or maximum over all subarrays by crediting each one to a single element, ties included.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mono/monotonic-deque-the-maximum-of-every-window":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a deque of indices whose values strictly decrease from front to back. A new value first drops every smaller or equal value from the back (they are older and no larger, so they can never be a window maximum again), then joins at the back; the front leaves when it slides out of the window. The front is always the window maximum, and each index enters and leaves once: O(n) total, O(k) space.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dominance.\u003c/b> If j &lt; i and a[j] &lt;= a[i], then j leaves every future window before i does and is never larger, so j is useless from now on. Removing all dominated indices leaves candidates with decreasing values, and the oldest surviving candidate is the largest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant.\u003c/b> After step i, the deque holds, in increasing index order, exactly the indices j in the window [i - k + 1, i] whose value is greater than every later value in the window. Their values strictly decrease, so the front is the maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expiry.\u003c/b> The window moves one step at a time, so at most one index, the oldest, expires per step: check \u003ccode>dq[0] &lt;= i - k\u003c/code> before reading the front.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> n appends, at most n pops from the back and n from the front: O(n). On random data almost every index leaves from the back; only about n/k expire from the front (the lab measures this).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Both extremes\u003c/b> (longest subarray whose max - min is within a limit): keep a decreasing deque for the maximum and an increasing one for the minimum under a grow-and-shrink window; while the fronts differ by more than the limit, advance the left edge and drop fronts that fall behind it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>On prefix sums\u003c/b> (shortest subarray with sum at least K, negatives allowed): keep start indices with increasing prefix sums. Pop the front while the current prefix minus it reaches K, recording the length (a later end could only be longer for that start), and pop the back while its prefix is at least the current one (a later, lower start is strictly better).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Alternatives.\u003c/b> A heap of \u003ccode>(-value, index)\u003c/code> with lazy deletion (pop the top while it has expired) is O(n log n) and hard to get wrong. A queue made of two max-stacks gives amortised O(1) per step. A sparse table answers arbitrary ranges on fixed data, not just sliding ones.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef window_max(a, k):\n    dq = deque()                        # indices in the window; values strictly decrease front to back\n    out = []\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        if dq and dq[0] &lt;= i - k:\n            dq.popleft()                # the front slid out of the window\n        while dq and a[dq[-1]] &lt;= x:\n            dq.pop()                    # older and not larger: can never be a maximum again\n        dq.append(i)\n        if i &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(a[dq[0]])\n    return out\n\n\ndef shortest_subarray_at_least(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Shortest non-empty subarray with sum &gt;= k (negatives allowed), or -1.\"\"\"\n    pre = [0]\n    for x in a:\n        pre.append(pre[-1] + x)\n    best, dq = len(a) + 1, deque()      # candidate starts j with increasing pre[j]\n    for i, p in enumerate(pre):\n        while dq and p - pre[dq[0]] &gt;= k:\n            best = min(best, i - dq.popleft())   # no later end can give this start a shorter answer\n        while dq and pre[dq[-1]] &gt;= p:\n            dq.pop()                    # i is a later and lower start: strictly better\n        dq.append(i)\n    return best if best &lt;= len(a) else -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">a = 9 7 8 3 6 2 5 1, k = 3. i = 0 and i = 1: 9 and 7 join; 9 &gt; 7, so both stay as candidates\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index    0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na        9   7   8   3   6   2   5   1\ndeque   [0:9, \u003cmark>1:7\u003c/mark>]           out []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2, x = 8: 7 &lt;= 8, so drop index 1 from the back; append 2. First full window [0, 2]: the front gives 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [0:9, \u003cmark>2:8\u003c/mark>]           out [\u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3, x = 3: index 0 is now outside the window [1, 3], so it expires from the front; append 3. Max 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [\u003cmark>2:8\u003c/mark>, 3:3]           out [9, \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, x = 6: drop index 3 (3 &lt;= 6) from the back; 8 &gt; 6 stays. Max of window [2, 4] is still 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [2:8, \u003cmark>4:6\u003c/mark>]           out [9, 8, \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5, x = 2: index 2 expires from the front; append 5. The next candidate, 6, becomes the maximum\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [\u003cmark>4:6\u003c/mark>, 5:2]           out [9, 8, 8, \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 6, x = 5: drop index 5 (2 &lt;= 5); 6 stays. Max 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [4:6, \u003cmark>6:5\u003c/mark>]           out [9, 8, 8, 6, \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7, x = 1: index 4 expires; append 7. Max 5. Eight appends, three drops, three expiries\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque   [\u003cmark>6:5\u003c/mark>, 7:1]           out [9, 8, 8, 6, 6, \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Storing values in the deque: you cannot tell when they expire. Store indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reading the front before expiring it, or emitting output before the first full window (\u003ccode>i &gt;= k - 1\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> when dropping from the back: both give correct maxima; \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> keeps fewer equal values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Using a plain sliding window when values can be negative: growing the window no longer only increases the sum, which is why the prefix-sum deque exists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace, why does index 2 (value 8) survive the arrival of 6 at i = 4, while index 3 (value 3) is dropped?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>3 is not larger than 6 and index 3 is older, so it can never be a window maximum again. 8 is larger than 6, so it is still the maximum of the window [2, 4]; it leaves only when it expires at i = 5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to maintain window maxima and minima with a monotonic deque, and how to extend it to prefix sums.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mono/recognising-monotonic-structure-and-when-a-heap-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reach for a monotonic stack when an element becomes useless \u003ci>forever\u003c/i> as soon as a better one arrives after it: a larger value for next greater, a smaller digit for the smallest number, a slower car ahead for fleets. If a discarded element could matter again, because items leave in arbitrary order or you need the k-th best rather than the best, the structure is not monotonic: use a heap or a sorted container.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>You read\u003c/th>\u003cth>Think\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>next or previous greater or smaller, days until warmer, consecutive days before\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic stack; answer at pop time or after popping\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>until a taller one blocks the view, visible people, skylines\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic stack scanned from the blocking side\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sum of min or max over all subarrays, largest rectangle\u003c/td>\u003ctd>nearest smaller on both sides, then contribution or width\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>maximum or minimum of every window, best of the last k states\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>remove k digits or letters for the smallest result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>increasing stack, pop larger items while removals remain\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>objects that cannot overtake (fleets, collisions)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort by position, keep a stack of groups\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Greedy pops.\u003c/b> For the smallest number after removing k digits, the most valuable removal is the first digit that is followed by a smaller one: deleting it lowers the most significant place that can change. The stack keeps a non-decreasing prefix, and each new digit pops larger digits while removals remain. Leftover removals come off the end, where the kept digits are largest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fleets.\u003c/b> Sort cars by position from the target backwards. A car whose solo arrival time is no later than the fleet in front catches it and merges; only a strictly later time starts a new fleet. Only the top of that stack is ever compared, so it collapses to one variable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When a heap is simpler.\u003c/b>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Items expire strictly oldest first (sliding windows)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>monotonic deque, O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Items leave in any order (intervals end, deletions by key)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>heap with lazy deletion, O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You need the k-th best or the top k, not just the best\u003c/td>\u003ctd>heap, or \u003ccode>sortedcontainers.SortedList\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Arbitrary range queries on fixed data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sparse table or segment tree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>n is small or the code must be obviously right\u003c/td>\u003ctd>brute force or a heap first, then optimise if measured\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_k_digits(num, k):\n    stack = []\n    for d in num:\n        while k and stack and stack[-1] &gt; d:\n            stack.pop()                 # a larger digit before a smaller one: drop it\n            k -= 1\n        stack.append(d)\n    if k:\n        stack = stack[:-k]              # still owe removals: the tail is non-decreasing, cut the end\n    return \"\".join(stack).lstrip(\"0\") or \"0\"\n\n\ndef car_fleet(target, position, speed):\n    fleets = 0\n    lead_d = lead_s = None              # the fleet ahead arrives at time lead_d / lead_s\n    for p, s in sorted(zip(position, speed), reverse=True):\n        d = target - p\n        if fleets == 0 or d * lead_s &gt; lead_d * s:   # arrives strictly later: cannot catch up\n            fleets += 1\n            lead_d, lead_s = d, s\n    return fleets\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove k = 3 digits from 5283419. Digit 5: the stack is empty, push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>       k 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 2: 5 &gt; 2, and removing the 5 makes the leading digit smaller, so pop it; push 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>       k 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 8: no larger digit to pop; push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   2 \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>     k 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 3: 8 &gt; 3, pop 8; 2 &lt; 3 stops; push 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   2 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>     k 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 4: push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   2 3 \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   k 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 1: 4 &gt; 1, pop it; now k is 0, so 3 stays even though it is larger; push 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   2 3 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   k 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Digit 9: push. No removals are owed, no leading zeros: the answer is 2319\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   2 3 1 \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark> k 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Removal problems: leftover k (cut from the end), leading zeros (strip them) and an empty result (return \"0\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>Floating-point arrival times can make equal times compare unequal; compare \u003ccode>d1 * s2\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>d2 * s1\u003c/code> in integers, as the template does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Applying a monotonic structure when the \"useless forever\" argument fails, such as the largest value among intervals that end in arbitrary order.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a monotonic deque not maintain the maximum of a set from which arbitrary elements are deleted?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It discards an element as soon as a larger, newer one arrives, which is safe only if the newer one is guaranteed to stay at least as long. With arbitrary deletions the newer element may be deleted first, and the discarded one would be needed again. A heap with lazy deletion, or a sorted container, keeps every element and copes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to recognise monotonic structure from a problem statement, and when to choose a heap instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:mono/count-the-pops-measuring-the-amortised-bound":"\u003cp>The \"while inside a for\" shape makes many people guess O(n^2). Here you count the operations instead: four input shapes, doubling sizes, and a brute-force oracle to prove the fast versions right first. If you built the \u003ccode>dsakit\u003c/code> stress runner in the correctness module, plug these functions into it; the code below is self-contained.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>count_pops.py\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nimport time\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef next_greater_counted(a):\n    ans, stack = [-1] * len(a), []\n    pushes = pops = 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        while stack and a[stack[-1]] &lt; x:\n            ans[stack.pop()] = x\n            pops += 1\n        stack.append(i)\n        pushes += 1\n    return ans, pushes, pops\n\n\ndef window_max_counted(a, k):\n    dq, out = deque(), []\n    pushes = drops = expiries = 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        if dq and dq[0] &lt;= i - k:\n            dq.popleft()\n            expiries += 1\n        while dq and a[dq[-1]] &lt;= x:\n            dq.pop()\n            drops += 1\n        dq.append(i)\n        pushes += 1\n        if i &gt;= k - 1:\n            out.append(a[dq[0]])\n    return out, pushes, drops, expiries\n\n\ndef next_greater_slow(a):\n    n = len(a)\n    return [next((a[j] for j in range(i + 1, n) if a[j] &gt; a[i]), -1) for i in range(n)]\n\n\ndef window_max_slow(a, k):\n    return [max(a[i:i + k]) for i in range(len(a) - k + 1)]\n\n\nINPUTS = {\n    \"random\": lambda n, rng: [rng.randint(0, 10**6) for _ in range(n)],\n    \"increasing\": lambda n, rng: list(range(n)),\n    \"decreasing\": lambda n, rng: list(range(n, 0, -1)),\n    \"all equal\": lambda n, rng: [7] * n,\n}\n\n\ndef stress(trials=3000, seed=1):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    for _ in range(trials):\n        n = rng.randint(0, 40)\n        a = [rng.randint(0, 5) for _ in range(n)]\n        assert next_greater_counted(a)[0] == next_greater_slow(a)\n        if n:\n            k = rng.randint(1, n)\n            assert window_max_counted(a, k)[0] == window_max_slow(a, k)\n    print(f\"stress: {trials} random arrays agree with the O(n^2) versions\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    stress()\n    rng = random.Random(0)\n    k = 100\n    print(f\"{'input':&gt;11} {'n':&gt;8} | NGE pushes   pops | window pushes  drops  expiries | NGE ms  window ms\")\n    for name, make in INPUTS.items():\n        for n in (100_000, 200_000, 400_000, 800_000):\n            a = make(n, rng)\n            t0 = time.perf_counter()\n            _, p1, q1 = next_greater_counted(a)\n            t1 = time.perf_counter()\n            _, p2, d2, e2 = window_max_counted(a, k)\n            t2 = time.perf_counter()\n            assert q1 &lt;= p1 == n and d2 + e2 &lt;= p2 == n\n            print(f\"{name:&gt;11} {n:&gt;8} | {p1:&gt;10} {q1:&gt;6} | {p2:&gt;13} {d2:&gt;6} {e2:&gt;9} | \"\n                  f\"{(t1 - t0) * 1e3:6.0f} {(t2 - t1) * 1e3:10.0f}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Before running it, write down your prediction of the pops for next greater and of the drops and expiries for the window, for each of the four input shapes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 count_pops.py\u003c/code>. The stress test must pass before you trust any count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Plot (or just tabulate) pushes plus pops against n for each shape, and the time ratio between consecutive sizes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Break the window code on purpose: move the expiry check after the \u003ccode>append\u003c/code>, and change \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> in the drop loop. Which change breaks correctness, and which only changes the counts? Use the stress test to check your answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: add a lazily deleted heap version of the window maximum and time it against the deque at n = 800,000.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Pushes always equal n, and pops (or drops plus expiries) never exceed n, on every shape: the amortised bound in numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Next greater: increasing input pops n - 1 times (every new value resolves the previous one); decreasing and all-equal input never pop; random input pops all but a handful (the elements left are the ones with nothing greater to their right, about ln n of them, roughly a dozen here).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Window maximum with k = 100: increasing and all-equal input drop n - 1 from the back and expire nothing; decreasing input drops nothing and expires n - k from the front; random input drops about 99% of indices and expires about n/k, because an element survives to expire only if it is the largest of the k values starting at it, which happens with probability about 1/k.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Time roughly doubles when n doubles (about 30 to 50 ms for next greater at 100,000 on the machine used here; single runs on a busy machine are noisy, so repeat and take the minimum). No column grows four-fold.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Moving the expiry check after the append is still correct (the newly appended index can never be the expired one when k is at least 1); changing \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> is also correct but keeps equal values, so on all-equal input nothing is dropped and n - k indices expire from the front instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">On decreasing input with k = 100, why do almost all indices leave through the front of the deque, and does that change the O(n) bound?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each new value is smaller than everything in the deque, so nothing is ever dropped from the back; indices leave only when they slide out of the window, n - k of them. The bound is unchanged: every index is still appended once and removed at most once, whichever end it leaves by.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:496":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a list of distinct numbers and a query list drawn from it, return, for each queried number, the first strictly larger number to its right in the original list. Use -1 if there is none. For \u003ccode>[4, 1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>, queries \u003ccode>[1, 4]\u003c/code> produce \u003ccode>[3, -1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The source values are distinct and every query occurs in the source. The standard limits allow up to 1,000 values in either list, with values from 0 to 10,000. Thus a value identifies one position unambiguously, and a dictionary can store its answer. The query order need not match source order. The sentinel -1 cannot be confused with a found value in this domain.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each value in a short query list, report the first larger value to its right in a longer list.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Answer the question once for every element of the longer list, then look the queries up.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (nearest greater or smaller)\u003c/p>\u003cp>One pass over the longer list with a stack of values still waiting; each new value pops every smaller waiting value and is its next greater. Store those answers in a dict (values are distinct) and map each query through it, defaulting to -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + m) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Locate each query and walk right until a larger value appears. In the worst case this costs O(mn) for m queries and n source values, even if a position map removes the search for each starting location. The answer list uses O(m) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process the source from left to right. Keep unresolved values in a stack whose values decrease from bottom to top. A new value resolves every smaller top entry: nothing between that entry and the current position was larger, or it would already have resolved the entry. A larger unresolved entry blocks the current value, and everything below it is larger still.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Source [4, 1, 3, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 4 and 1; waiting [4, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">See 3: pop 1 and record 1 -&gt; 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 3, then 2; waiting [4, 3, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">End: remaining values have no answer\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queries [1, 4] yield \u003cmark>[3, -1]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>By induction the stack contains exactly the seen values without a larger seen successor. Every pop is resolved at the earliest possible index. Unpopped entries have no larger successor anywhere, so their default is correct.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def next_greater_queries(queries, nums):\n    waiting = []\n    answer = {}\n    for value in nums:\n        while waiting and waiting[-1] &lt; value:\n            answer[waiting.pop()] = value\n        waiting.append(value)\n    return [answer.get(value, -1) for value in queries]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each source value is pushed once and popped at most once. Expected time is O(n + m) with dictionary operations; auxiliary storage is O(n), plus O(m) for the returned list. The input lists are unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Greater means strict; equality would not resolve a waiting value, although the stated source is distinct.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mapping by value breaks when the source has duplicates because the same value can have different successors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty query list returns an empty list; a decreasing source produces only -1 answers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return successor indices instead, using a stack of indices rather than values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If duplicates are allowed, query positions rather than values and retain one answer per index.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:739":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each day's temperature, return the distance to the earliest later day with a strictly higher temperature. Return zero for days without such a later day. For \u003ccode>[70, 72, 71, 73]\u003c/code>, the waits are \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1, 0]\u003c/code>. The value reported is a number of days, not the warmer temperature.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard input has up to 100,000 days, with temperatures between 30 and 100. Entries can tie, and tied temperatures do not qualify as warmer. Scanning all later days for every position is wasteful at that scale. Integer comparisons are enough, and indices are needed to calculate distances. This implementation also accepts an empty sequence.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each day, how many days until a strictly warmer one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Think about the days that have not found a warmer day yet. In what order are their temperatures?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (nearest greater or smaller)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a stack of indices of days still waiting; their temperatures never increase upwards. A warmer day i pops each cooler waiting day j and sets answer[j] = i - j; days never popped keep 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each index, inspect following entries in order until the first warmer temperature appears; if none does, write zero. A decreasing or constant sequence causes n(n-1)/2 comparisons, giving O(n²) time and O(n) output space. This is a useful independent specification, but it repeats work on the same suffixes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep indices of unresolved days in a stack. Their temperatures are nonincreasing from bottom to top. When the current temperature is higher than the top, it is that day's first warmer successor: any earlier warmer day would have removed it already. Set the distance and continue popping, since the current day can resolve several older days at once. Push the current index after resolving earlier days.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If a top day is not cooler than today, it stays. All entries underneath are at least as warm, so they cannot be resolved by today either. Equal entries must remain separately because they may have different distances to a future warmer day.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Temperatures [70, 72, 71, 73]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>day 0 waits; day 1 resolves it at distance 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Day 2 is cooler than day 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack indices [1, 2], both unresolved\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Day 3 resolves day 2 at distance 1 and day 1 at distance 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remaining day 3 keeps zero; result \u003cmark>[1, 2, 1, 0]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Initially the stack is empty and every answer is zero. At each step, the pop rule establishes the first warmer day for exactly the entries it removes; the rest still have no warmer day in the processed prefix. At termination unresolved entries have no warmer day anywhere, proving their zero answers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def daily_temperatures(temperatures):\n    result = [0] * len(temperatures)\n    waiting = []\n    for day, temp in enumerate(temperatures):\n        while waiting and temperatures[waiting[-1]] &lt; temp:\n            previous = waiting.pop()\n            result[previous] = day - previous\n        waiting.append(day)\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every index enters and leaves the stack at most once, even though a single day can trigger many pops. The total running time is O(n), auxiliary stack space is O(n), and the returned array occupies O(n). No temperatures are changed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code>: equal temperatures do not end a wait.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store indices, not only temperatures, so the distance is available.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The last day and all days in a nonincreasing sequence correctly retain zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A long sequence of equal temperatures is legal and can all be resolved by one later warmer day.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the next warmer temperature instead of the distance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Require a temperature at least as high as today's; change the comparison and revisit equality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:503":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each position in a circular array, find the first strictly greater value encountered by moving right and wrapping at the end. Use -1 when no such value exists. For \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2, 4]\u003c/code>, the answers are \u003ccode>[4, 2, 4, -1]\u003c/code>. Each position is a separate query even if values repeat.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard input has up to 10,000 values, each between -10⁹ and 10⁹. Values may repeat, so values alone cannot identify waiting positions. One full circuit after an index examines every other position; going farther cannot discover a new candidate. Empty input returns an empty result as an extension. A found value can itself be -1, so the prescribed -1 output cannot distinguish that answer from absence.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The next larger value for each element when the array wraps around at the end.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Wrapping means an element's answer may lie before it. How far past the end do you need to look?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (nearest greater or smaller)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run the usual next-greater pass over i from 0 to 2n - 1 using a[i % n], but push indices only during the first lap; the second lap only resolves elements still waiting. Whatever is never popped (the maximum values) keeps -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every index, examine offsets 1 through n-1, using modular indexing, until finding a greater value. This takes O(n²) time in a constant or decreasing-like circular input, and O(n) for the answers. Its direct order of inspection also makes it a good oracle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Imagine scanning two copies of the array consecutively, but put each index on the waiting stack only in the first copy. Each virtual position can resolve earlier waiting entries whose values are strictly smaller. A second copy exposes the wrapped part of each search; a third adds no new information. The stack is nonincreasing by value, so once its top cannot be resolved neither can any element below it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Although an unresolved index can still be present when its own second-copy value appears, it cannot pop itself: equality is not greater. If it has no greater value, it stays unresolved and its default remains -1. Store indices so equal values get independent answers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Array [2, 1, 2, 4], initial answers all -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first 2 waits; 1 waits; next 2 resolves 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">4 resolves both 2s and waits itself\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answers [4, 2, 4, -1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The second lap has no value larger than 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>[4, 2, 4, -1]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every pop occurs at the first greater virtual position after its original index, because earlier candidates would already have popped it. The two scans include all possible circular successors. Consequently entries never popped have no valid greater element.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def next_greater_circular(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    answer = [-1] * n\n    waiting = []\n    for i in range(2 * n):\n        value = nums[i % n]\n        while waiting and nums[waiting[-1]] &lt; value:\n            answer[waiting.pop()] = value\n        if i &lt; n:\n            waiting.append(i)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The loop visits 2n virtual positions. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so time is O(n). The stack and output each use O(n) space. For n=0 the loop never evaluates the modulo operation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Pushing on both laps duplicates work and can overwrite an answer with a non-first successor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not pop equal values; an all-equal array returns all -1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A singleton has no strictly greater successor, including itself.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Looking only to the physical end misses valid wrapped answers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the circular distance to the next greater position rather than its value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the predicate to next strictly smaller, and reverse the stack comparisons.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:901":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Prices arrive in chronological order. After each arrival return the number of consecutive days ending today whose prices are no greater than today's price. For arrivals \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2, 2]\u003c/code>, spans are \u003ccode>[1, 1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>. The count includes today and stops just after the nearest earlier strictly higher price.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard constraints allow at most 10,000 calls, with positive prices up to 100,000. An online interface must answer without knowing future prices. Equal prices count as covered, and a long increasing run may make the span as large as the number of calls. A data structure may retain history between calls; it need not store every individual day if it can summarize groups without losing future answers.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prices arrive one at a time; for each, report how many consecutive days up to today had a price no higher than today's.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Once a higher price arrives, the cheaper days just before it are covered by it for every future query.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (nearest greater or smaller)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep (price, span) pairs with strictly decreasing prices. For a new price, pop every pair whose price is at most today's and add its span to today's span (which starts at 1), then push (price, span). Each day is pushed and popped once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) amortised per call, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Append each price to a list and scan backward while entries are no greater than it. One call can inspect the whole history; across n increasing prices the total is O(n²), with O(n) storage. Repeatedly visiting the same covered days is the source of the wasted work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store pairs \u003ccode>(price, span)\u003c/code> in a stack with strictly decreasing prices. A pair's span represents a contiguous block ending at that price; all earlier prices within that block are at most the pair's price. If a new price is at least the top price, the new span can absorb the entire top block. Pop and add its span, repeating until the remaining top is strictly higher or the stack is empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The retained higher price is exactly the barrier that stops today's span. Any popped pair can never again be a better barrier for a future day: the newer price is at least as high and lies closer to that future day. The aggregated count preserves how many days the new pair covers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Receive 3 then 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [(3, 1), (1, 1)], spans 1 and 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Receive 2: absorb (1, 1), stop at 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push (2, 2), report 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Receive 2: equal top is absorbed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push (2, 3), report \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Inductively each stack pair is a block of consecutive covered days, and the blocks partition the processed history. Absorbing exactly the consecutive suffix of blocks with top prices at most the new price yields precisely the maximal valid suffix ending today. Remaining prices stay strictly decreasing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class StockSpanner:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []\n\n    def next(self, price):\n        span = 1\n        while self.stack and self.stack[-1][0] &lt;= price:\n            span += self.stack.pop()[1]\n        self.stack.append((price, span))\n        return span\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each call pushes one pair, and every pair can be popped only once. Over n calls time is O(n), or O(1) amortized per call, though one call may take O(n). The stack occupies O(n) space in a decreasing stream.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Pop equal prices as well as smaller ones, because both satisfy the span condition.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the whole stored span, not just one per pop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Initialize every new span to one for the current day.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The first price has span one, and a strictly decreasing stream keeps all pairs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Ask for the closest earlier strictly higher price instead of the number of covered days.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep original day indices to return the left boundary as well as the span.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:402":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Remove exactly k digits from a nonnegative integer written as a digit string. Preserve the relative order of the remaining digits and return the smallest resulting number as a string, without leading zeros. If no nonzero digit remains, return \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>\"1432\"\u003c/code> and k=2, choosing the subsequence \u003ccode>\"12\"\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>\"12\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume \u003ccode>0 &lt;= k &lt;= len(num)\u003c/code> and that the input contains decimal digits; the standard length limit is 100,000. Standard inputs have no leading zeros except for \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code>, though the function also normalizes leading zeros on other inputs. The number can be too long for convenient integer conversion, so compare digit characters and build a string. Every candidate has the same number of retained digits before normalization; lexicographic order therefore agrees with numerical order, including candidates beginning with zeros.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Delete exactly k digits from a number written as a string so the result is as small as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compare two neighbouring digits. When is deleting the left one clearly the best possible move?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy pops on a monotonic stack\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan with a stack kept non-decreasing: while removals remain and the top digit is greater than the new digit, pop it. If removals are still owed at the end, cut them from the end. Strip leading zeros and return \"0\" if nothing is left.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every choice of k deleted positions, retain the others in order, and take the smallest normalized number. There are binomial(n, k) choices, each requiring O(n) work and O(n) working space. The approach is useful on short strings, but the number of choices grows rapidly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For two adjacent retained candidates where the earlier digit is larger, deleting that earlier digit improves the first position where the resulting strings differ. Maintain a stack of selected digits. When a smaller digit arrives and deletions remain, pop larger digits from the end until that inversion disappears or the budget is exhausted. Then append the new digit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This exchange is safe: replacing a larger earlier digit by the smaller current one improves the earliest affected position, while leaving the relative order of all other kept digits intact. Applying the exchange repeatedly gives the smallest possible prefix for the spent budget. If the scan ends with unused deletions, the remaining stack is nondecreasing; removing its final digits retains the smallest possible prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">num = \"1432\", k = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 1, then 4; stack 14\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 3: pop 4, spend one deletion, push 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack 13, one deletion remains\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 2: pop 3 and push 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack 12; normalized answer \u003cmark>\"12\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Any optimal solution with a larger digit immediately before a smaller available digit can be exchanged for one deleting the larger digit, unless earlier budget choices already determine the prefix. The stack makes these exchanges as early as possible. After all inversions that the budget permits are repaired, deleting the suffix is optimal, so exactly k digits are removed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_k_digits(num, k):\n    stack = []\n    remaining = k\n    for digit in num:\n        while remaining and stack and stack[-1] &gt; digit:\n            stack.pop()\n            remaining -= 1\n        stack.append(digit)\n    if remaining:\n        del stack[-remaining:]\n    return \"\".join(stack).lstrip(\"0\") or \"0\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each digit is pushed once and popped at most once. Joining and stripping take O(n), giving O(n) time and O(n) stack/output space. The function does not convert the potentially long number to an integer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Remove exactly k digits before stripping leading zeros; normalization is not an extra deletion decision.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Guard the suffix deletion: \u003ccode>stack[-0:]\u003c/code> denotes the entire stack, not an empty suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equal digits need not be popped; preserving the earlier one leaves the same prefix with more options later.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With k equal to n, the result is \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code>; k=0 still normalizes leading zeros.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Maximize the remaining number by reversing the comparison.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return retained indices alongside the normalized value, keeping normalization separate from selection.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:853":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cars start at distinct positions before a target, each with a positive constant speed until it catches a slower group ahead. Cars cannot pass; after catching, they travel together at the group's speed. Count the groups arriving at the target, including a catch exactly at the target. For target 12, positions \u003ccode>[10, 8, 0]\u003c/code> and speeds \u003ccode>[2, 4, 1]\u003c/code>, the first two meet at the target and the answer is 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard limits permit up to 100,000 cars and a target up to 1,000,000. Positions are distinct, nonnegative, and strictly below the target; speeds are positive integers up to 1,000,000. Each position corresponds to its speed, and the input need not be sorted. A car's independent arrival time is a positive rational number. Comparing ratios by cross multiplication avoids floating-point rounding near a tie; Python integers do not overflow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Cars on a single lane cannot pass each other; count the groups that reach the target.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Process cars starting from the one closest to the target. When does a car end up in the group ahead of it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy pops on a monotonic stack\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by position, closest to the target first, and compute each car's arrival time if it drove alone. A car whose time is no later than the fleet ahead catches it and merges; a strictly later time starts a new fleet. Only the latest fleet's time matters; compare d1 * s2 with d2 * s1 to avoid float ties.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time for the sort, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the cars from front to back, then simulate moving groups and their next catch events until all reach the destination. Alternatively, compare a rear car's path against each group already constructed. Pairwise group scans can take O(n²) after sorting and require O(n) storage. The group immediately ahead is enough, so the extra comparisons are avoidable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process positions from nearest to farthest. A group ahead has an effective arrival time: the independent time of the slowest limiting car in that group. A following car with independent time no greater than that group's time catches the group by the target, including equality, and inherits its arrival time. A car with a strictly later independent time cannot catch that group and starts a new group behind it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Only the arrival time of the most recently formed group matters. If the new car cannot catch this nearest group, it cannot pass it to reach any group farther ahead. If it catches, the nearest group's arrival time remains unchanged, so no additional state is needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">target 12, cars ordered by position 10, 8, 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>front car time 2/2 = 1; start fleet 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Car at 8 has time 4/4 = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>catches at target; fleet count stays 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Car at 0 has time 12/1 = 12 &gt; 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>starts a second fleet; answer \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The processed cars form valid fleets in front-to-back order. The nearest fleet acts as a barrier to all earlier fleets. Comparing arrival times determines whether the next car reaches that barrier by the destination, so induction preserves both the fleet count and the last fleet's arrival time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def car_fleets(target, positions, speeds):\n    cars = sorted(zip(positions, speeds), reverse=True)\n    fleets = 0\n    last_distance, last_speed = 0, 1\n    for position, speed in cars:\n        distance = target - position\n        if distance * last_speed &gt; last_distance * speed:\n            fleets += 1\n            last_distance, last_speed = distance, speed\n    return fleets\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting n cars costs O(n log n) time and O(n) space for the sorted pairs. The scan is O(n), using constant additional state beyond sorting. Exact integer arithmetic assumes the usual unit-cost comparison model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Equal arrival times merge: use strict greater-than for a new fleet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Process the closest car first; the reverse direction lacks the fleet barrier information.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the front fleet's arrival time after a merge, not the following car's shorter time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No cars means zero fleets; positive speed and positions before the target are assumed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the members of each fleet by storing a list for each newly formed group.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If bounded integer positions permit counting sort, trade extra positional storage for faster ordering.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:907":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every nonempty contiguous subarray, take its minimum and add those minima. Return the sum modulo 1,000,000,007. For \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2]\u003c/code>, the six minima are 3, 1, 1, 1, 1, and 2, yielding 9. Equal values are separate occurrences, even when the resulting minima match.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard input has at most 30,000 positive integers, each at most 30,000, so listing all subarrays is too expensive. The unreduced total may be large. Python supports arbitrary-size integers, and applying the modulus to the final sum gives the required residue. Empty input can be interpreted as a sum of zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add up the minimum of every contiguous subarray, with n in the tens of thousands.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Flip the sum around: for one element, in how many subarrays is it the minimum?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Contribution counting with nearest smaller\u003c/p>\u003cp>a[i] is the credited minimum of every subarray that starts after its previous strictly smaller element and ends before its next smaller-or-equal element, so it adds a[i] * (i - L) * (R - i). Find L and R with two monotonic stack passes; the mixed strictness counts tied minima once. Reduce modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose a left endpoint, extend the right endpoint one position at a time, and maintain the running minimum. Add it at each extension. This visits n(n+1)/2 subarrays in O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space, excluding the input. It is straightforward enough to serve as an independent test oracle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reverse the counting: assign each subarray to one occurrence of its minimum, and count the subarrays assigned to each index. Choose the rightmost occurrence when equal minima occur. For index i, find the previous strictly smaller value at L and the next smaller-or-equal value at R, using -1 and n if absent. Any left endpoint from L+1 through i and right endpoint from i through R-1 makes i the rightmost minimum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>(i-L) * (R-i)\u003c/code> such endpoint pairs, so i contributes its value times that product. The asymmetry matters: an equal minimum to the left is allowed, but an equal minimum to the right takes ownership. Thus every subarray has exactly one owner rather than being counted twice or omitted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Array [3, 1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>previous strictly smaller indices L = [-1, -1, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Next smaller-or-equal indices R = [1, 3, 3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>contributions: 3*1*1, 1*2*2, 2*1*1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sum 3 + 4 + 2 = 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>residue \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In the left pass, pop values greater than or equal to the current value; the remaining top is the closest strictly smaller index. In the right pass, pop only greater values; the remaining top is the closest smaller-or-equal index. The endpoint rectangle defined by those boundaries is exactly the set owned by i, proving the contribution formula and the sum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def sum_subarray_mins(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    left = [-1] * n\n    right = [n] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] &gt;= value:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            left[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] &gt; nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            right[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n    total = sum(value * (i - left[i]) * (right[i] - i)\n                for i, value in enumerate(nums))\n    return total % 1_000_000_007\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each index is pushed and popped at most once in each pass. Time is O(n); the boundary arrays and stack use O(n) auxiliary space. Modulo arithmetic does not change the asymptotic operation count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Using strict comparisons on both sides double-counts intervals with equal minima; using non-strict boundaries on both sides misses some.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The boundary positions themselves are excluded, hence the endpoint counts use distances rather than distances plus one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>On \u003ccode>[2, 2]\u003c/code>, the rightmost 2 owns the two-element subarray; the sum is 6.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty input yields zero, and one element contributes itself modulo the modulus.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sum subarray maxima by reversing the comparisons and choosing a consistent tie owner.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return each index's contribution separately to inspect the partition of subarrays.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:84":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A histogram consists of adjacent unit-width bars with nonnegative heights. Find the largest area of a rectangle lying entirely under the bar tops. A rectangle spans a consecutive run of bars and its height cannot exceed any bar in that run. For heights \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2]\u003c/code>, the best area is 3, achieved by spanning all three bars at height 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard limit is 100,000 bars, each of height 0 through 10,000. Bars can have equal heights and zeros. Width is the number of consecutive bars, not the difference between bar heights. An empty histogram has area zero as an extension. Since a rectangle can be lowered until its top meets a shortest bar in its span, considering areas associated with bar heights suffices; arbitrary real heights need not be enumerated.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest rectangle that fits under a row of adjacent bars of different heights.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix which bar is the shortest one in the rectangle. How far can the rectangle spread from it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Rising stack of bars (histogram)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a stack of bar indices with increasing heights and append a height-0 sentinel. When bar i pops index t, i is t's right limit and the new top is its left limit, so the area is heights[t] * (i - left - 1) with left = -1 for an empty stack; keep the maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each left endpoint, extend a right endpoint across the histogram, maintaining the smallest height seen. The best rectangle for that span has area \u003ccode>minimum * width\u003c/code>. Taking the maximum over every span takes O(n²) time and O(1) working space. It independently checks the optimized method for small examples, including repeated heights.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a stack of indices whose heights are nondecreasing. When the current height is lower than the height at the top, pop that bar: the current index is the first position to the right that prevents a rectangle at the popped height. The new stack top is a left boundary at height no greater than the popped height, or -1 if none remains. The rectangle between these boundaries has width \u003ccode>right - left - 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The left boundary can be equal, not strictly smaller. This choice avoids popping equal bars during the scan. When equal bars are eventually popped from right to left, the leftmost equal bar gets the full width that includes the whole equal-height plateau. Thus at least one bar representing any optimum minimum receives enough width, even though other equal bars may receive smaller widths.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Process a virtual height zero after the last real bar. It flushes every positive height still on the stack, providing a right boundary at n. Zero-height bars may remain, but they cannot improve an area. The sentinel is virtual, so the input is not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Heights [2, 1, 2], best = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push index 0; height 1 pops it, width 1, area 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push index 1 then index 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack heights [1, 2], best 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Virtual zero at index 3 pops height 2: area 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>then pops height 1: width 3, area 3; result \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stack invariant holds initially and after each push: when a lower height arrives, all taller entries are removed before the current index is added. Every popped rectangle is valid because no intervening bar is lower than its height. Conversely, take an optimal span and its leftmost minimum bar. Before the first lower bar to its right, it stays on the stack; after taller bars are removed, the boundary to its left cannot cut into the span because no bar there is lower and this is the leftmost minimum. Its computed rectangle covers that span. Therefore the maximum of the candidates is optimal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def largest_rectangle(heights):\n    stack = []\n    best = 0\n    n = len(heights)\n    for right in range(n + 1):\n        current = heights[right] if right &lt; n else 0\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] &gt; current:\n            height = heights[stack.pop()]\n            left = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n            best = max(best, height * (right - left - 1))\n        if right &lt; n:\n            stack.append(right)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The loop visits n real bars and one sentinel. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so total time is O(n), despite the nested while loop. The stack takes O(n) auxiliary space for an increasing or equal-height sequence. The input is unchanged and the output is a single integer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The right boundary is excluded, as is the surviving left boundary; the width is not \u003ccode>right - left\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A final flush is necessary for bars that never meet a shorter real bar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equality requires a consistent policy. This implementation keeps equal bars and lets the leftmost one span their plateau.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A zero height breaks positive rectangles; all zeros and the empty list return zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This implementation assumes nonnegative heights; a zero sentinel would not flush negative heights.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a maximizing interval as well as its area by recording \u003ccode>left + 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>right - 1\u003c/code> on improvement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For variable positive bar widths, store cumulative horizontal coordinates and compute geometric width between boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:239":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an array and a window length k, report the maximum in every contiguous window of exactly k elements, from left to right. For \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2, 5]\u003c/code> and k=2, the windows have maxima \u003ccode>[3, 3, 5]\u003c/code>. A window advances by one index at a time, and equal values are valid.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard input contains up to 100,000 elements between -10,000 and 10,000. Assume \u003ccode>1 &lt;= k &lt;= len(nums)\u003c/code>: there is at least one complete window. The function raises \u003ccode>ValueError\u003c/code> for other k values, including an empty array. Values may be negative or repeated, so neither zero nor a value alone can mark an expired entry. The result has exactly n-k+1 elements.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The maximum of each window of length k as it slides one position at a time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If an element is older than another and no larger, can it ever be a window's maximum again?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic deque (window extremes)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a deque of indices with strictly decreasing values. Drop the front when it leaves the window, drop from the back while values are at most the new one, append the new index; once i &gt;= k - 1 the front holds the window maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(k) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each window, inspect all k values and record the greatest. This requires O((n-k+1)k) time and O(n-k+1) output space, with O(1) extra working space. It treats each window independently and repeats comparisons for the k-1 entries shared by adjacent windows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a deque of candidate indices in increasing index order and strictly decreasing value order. Before inserting index i, remove expired indices from the front: any index at most i-k lies outside the window ending at i. Then remove indices from the back while their values are at most the new value. The new index is later and no smaller, so any future window containing the older candidate also contains the newer one until the older expires. The older candidate can never be needed as a maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Append the new index. Once a full window exists, the deque's front has the largest retained value. Removed candidates are either outside the current window or dominated by a later candidate. Repeated equal values are safe to remove from the back because the newer equal value has the same maximum value and expires later.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The deque stores only indices in the current window. Removing from both ends is important: expiry happens by age at the front, while domination happens by value at the back. A plain stack cannot perform both operations efficiently.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, with window size three and values \u003ccode>[5, 5, 1, 2]\u003c/code>, the second 5 replaces the first as a candidate. It remains available for the second window after the older 5 has expired, so both windows correctly report 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums [1, 3, 2, 5], k = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>insert 1; insert 3 and discard 1; first maximum 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 2: deque indices [1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window [3, 2] has maximum 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Index 1 expires; 5 dominates 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque [3], final maximum 5; result \u003cmark>[3, 3, 5]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After each insertion, indices are current and ordered, and values decrease because all no-larger suffix entries were removed. Every discarded in-window value has a later retained dominator, possibly through a chain of replacements; as long as it remains in a window, that dominator also remains. Hence the largest window value is represented and must be at the front. Output begins only after k values have been processed, giving exactly one result per valid window.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef sliding_window_max(nums, k):\n    if not 1 &lt;= k &lt;= len(nums):\n        raise ValueError(\"k must be between 1 and the array length\")\n    candidates = deque()\n    answer = []\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        while candidates and candidates[0] &lt;= i - k:\n            candidates.popleft()\n        while candidates and nums[candidates[-1]] &lt;= value:\n            candidates.pop()\n        candidates.append(i)\n        if i &gt;= k - 1:\n            answer.append(nums[candidates[0]])\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each index is appended once and removed at most once, from either end. Total time is O(n), even if a single arrival removes many candidates. The deque holds at most k indices, so auxiliary space is O(k); the returned list requires O(n-k+1) space. The input is not changed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store indices rather than just values, so expiry is determined correctly when duplicates occur.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Drop index \u003ccode>i-k\u003c/code> before reading the maximum; it is just outside the new window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not emit a result until the first full window ends at k-1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With k=1 the answer copies the input; with k=n there is exactly one maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keeping equal values is also possible, but then expiry and the claimed deque ordering must match that choice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute window minima by reversing the value comparison.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Report the index of the maximum; specify whether the oldest or newest equal maximum wins before choosing the equality rule.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1438":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the greatest length of a contiguous subarray whose maximum minus minimum is at most a given nonnegative limit. For \u003ccode>[8, 2, 4, 7]\u003c/code> with limit 4, \u003ccode>[2, 4]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[4, 7]\u003c/code> are valid, and the answer is 2. Individual elements always form valid windows under the stated limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard input has up to 100,000 positive values, each at most 10⁹, and a limit from zero to 10⁹. This implementation also handles negative values and empty input. Duplicate values are allowed. A nonnegative limit makes the validity condition meaningful for a singleton. The window endpoints move only forward, because deleting elements cannot increase its maximum-minus-minimum range. Recomputing both extrema after each boundary move would lose the linear bound.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest contiguous stretch in which the largest and smallest values differ by at most a limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A grow-and-shrink window works if you can read the window's maximum and minimum quickly at every step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic deque (window extremes)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a decreasing deque for the maximum and an increasing deque for the minimum. Extend the right edge; while front(max) - front(min) exceeds the limit, advance the left edge and drop deque fronts that fall behind it. Record the window length at each step.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose each left endpoint and extend right while tracking its minimum and maximum. Test every span and keep the largest valid length. This takes O(n²) time and O(1) working space, and is suitable as a small-input oracle. Testing only neighboring differences is insufficient because the true extremes can be far apart.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain two deques of indices inside the current window: decreasing values for the maximum and increasing values for the minimum. On insertion, drop older no-better candidates from each back, then append the new index. Their fronts give the current extrema. If the range exceeds the limit, advance the left edge and remove any deque fronts that leave the window; repeat until valid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a fixed right endpoint, the first valid left endpoint after shrinking gives the longest valid window ending there. Any earlier left endpoint was invalid and remains invalid when more elements are added. Therefore scanning all right endpoints while never moving left backward still examines a longest candidate for each endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums [8, 2, 4, 7], limit 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[8] is valid; adding 2 makes range 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 8 from the window; [2, 4] has range 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best length becomes 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Adding 7 makes [2, 4, 7] invalid\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove 2; [4, 7] is valid, result \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each deque retains undominated in-window indices, so its front is the correct extremum. Shrinking terminates because a singleton has range zero. At each right endpoint the algorithm maintains the earliest feasible left boundary; the maximum recorded length is thus globally optimal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef longest_bounded_range(nums, limit):\n    if limit &lt; 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"limit must be nonnegative\")\n    high, low = deque(), deque()\n    left = best = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n        while high and nums[high[-1]] &lt;= value:\n            high.pop()\n        while low and nums[low[-1]] &gt;= value:\n            low.pop()\n        high.append(right)\n        low.append(right)\n        while nums[high[0]] - nums[low[0]] &gt; limit:\n            if high[0] == left:\n                high.popleft()\n            if low[0] == left:\n                low.popleft()\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each index enters and leaves each deque at most once, and the left boundary advances at most n times. Total time is O(n), with O(n) worst-case auxiliary space. The input remains unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Expire indices in both deques independently; an index can be both extrema.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Shrink in a loop, not just once, because several leftmost elements may need removal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Duplicate extrema are handled by keeping the newer equal candidate, which expires later.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With limit zero, the answer is the longest contiguous run of equal values.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the start and end indices of a longest valid window, with a stated tie rule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count all valid subarrays by adding the current valid window length at every right endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:862":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the minimum length of a nonempty contiguous subarray whose sum is at least a positive target k. Elements may be negative. Return -1 if no such subarray exists. For \u003ccode>[2, -1, 2]\u003c/code> and k=3, the whole array qualifies and the answer is 3; no shorter span does.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The standard limits allow 100,000 values in the range -100,000 to 100,000 and a positive target up to 10⁹. A simple grow-until-large, shrink-until-small window is not reliable: adding a negative number can decrease the sum, and removing one can increase it. Prefix sums allow any subarray sum to be computed as a difference, without assuming monotonicity of the original values. An empty array contains no nonempty candidate.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shortest subarray whose sum reaches a target when values can be negative.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A plain sliding window fails with negatives. Rewrite subarray sums with prefix sums: which earlier prefixes are still worth remembering?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic deque (window extremes)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Over prefix sums P, keep a deque of start indices with increasing P. For each i, pop the front while P[i] - P[front] &gt;= K (record i - front; later ends would only be longer), pop the back while P[back] &gt;= P[i] (a later, lower start dominates), then append i.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each start position, accumulate sums while extending the end and record the shortest qualifying length. Inspect all ends, because a negative value can make the sum decrease after it first crosses k. This takes O(n²) time and O(1) working space. It is an independent way to verify small cases.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Define \u003ccode>prefix[i]\u003c/code> as the sum of the first i values. The sum of \u003ccode>[j, i)\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>prefix[i] - prefix[j]\u003c/code>, and its length is i-j. As i moves from 0 to n, maintain a deque of possible starting prefix indices in increasing index order and strictly increasing prefix-sum order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>First, while the front yields a sum of at least k, record its length and remove it. Any future end paired with that same start will be farther away, so it cannot improve on the valid length just found. A later start at the next deque position may also qualify and gives a shorter length, so continue until the front no longer qualifies.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Then remove from the back every index j whose prefix sum is at least the current prefix sum. The current index is later and no higher: for every future end, it offers a sum at least as large and a strictly shorter length than j. Thus j is dominated. Append i only after checking earlier starts, so the algorithm never accepts an empty interval even for a nonpositive target.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums [2, -1, 2], k = 3; prefix sums [0, 2, 1, 3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>insert prefix index 0, then index 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At index 2, prefix 1 dominates index 1's prefix 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deque indices become [0, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At index 3, 3 - 0 reaches target\u003c/p>\u003cpre>record length 3, remove index 0; result \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>By induction, the deque contains undominated starts that have not already produced a qualifying interval at an earlier end. Removing a dominated start cannot lose an optimal interval: replacing it with its later, no-higher prefix gives a shorter valid interval for the same future end. Removing a qualifying front is also safe, because this end is its earliest qualifying end among the endpoints processed while it was retained, and later ends are longer. If the front fails the threshold, all later deque prefixes have larger sums and fail too. Therefore every potentially optimal interval is either recorded or improved by a retained alternative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef shortest_subarray_at_least(nums, k):\n    prefix = [0]\n    for value in nums:\n        prefix.append(prefix[-1] + value)\n    starts = deque()\n    best = len(nums) + 1\n    for end, total in enumerate(prefix):\n        while starts and total - prefix[starts[0]] &gt;= k:\n            best = min(best, end - starts.popleft())\n        while starts and prefix[starts[-1]] &gt;= total:\n            starts.pop()\n        starts.append(end)\n    return best if best &lt;= len(nums) else -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building n+1 prefix sums takes O(n) time and space. Every prefix index enters the deque once and leaves at most once, so the scan is also O(n) time and O(n) space. Python integer arithmetic avoids fixed-width sum overflow; fixed-width implementations need a sufficiently wide type.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start with prefix sum zero, or subarrays beginning at index zero are missed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The front loop must run before inserting the current prefix, to ensure a positive length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Remove equal prefix sums from the back: the later equal sum dominates the earlier one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A negative element can invalidate ordinary sliding-window reasoning even if the total of the full array is positive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return -1 when no qualifying interval exists; a single large element gives length one.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return interval endpoints by saving the start and end when the best length improves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the shortest subarray with sum at most a negative threshold by negating all values and the threshold.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:85":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a rectangular grid of \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"1\"\u003c/code> characters, return the largest area of an axis-aligned rectangle containing only ones. A rectangle must use consecutive rows and consecutive columns. For \u003ccode>[[\"1\", \"0\", \"1\"], [\"1\", \"1\", \"1\"]]\u003c/code>, the largest area is 3, using the whole second row.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume each row has the same number of columns and every cell is the string \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"1\"\u003c/code>; the standard grid has at most 200 rows and 200 columns. Either dimension may be zero in this implementation, in which case the answer is zero. Enumerating all rectangles and inspecting every cell is expensive. The result is an area, not a count of all possible rectangles.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest rectangle containing only 1s in a binary grid.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at the grid one row at a time. What does a row look like together with the 1s stacked above it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Rising stack of bars (histogram)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep heights[c] = the number of consecutive 1s ending at the current row in column c (reset to 0 on a 0). Each row is then a histogram, so run the largest-rectangle stack pass on it and keep the maximum over all rows.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(rows * cols) time, O(cols) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate top, bottom, left, and right boundaries, and check whether every cell inside each rectangle is one. On an r by c grid there are O(r²c²) boundary choices and checking each costs up to O(rc), for O(r³c³) time and O(1) extra space. A two-dimensional prefix sum can speed the validity checks, but still considers quadratically many boundary pairs in each dimension.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process one row at a time. At column c, keep the number of consecutive ones ending at the current row. A zero resets that height to zero, while a one extends it by one. These heights form a histogram. Every all-one rectangle whose bottom is the current row corresponds to consecutive histogram bars whose heights are at least the rectangle's height, and conversely every rectangle under this histogram represents a valid all-one grid rectangle.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the largest histogram rectangle with a nondecreasing stack of indices. When a lower bar arrives, the popped height cannot extend past that bar on the right; after the pop, the new top is its left boundary. Equal heights remain on the stack, so the leftmost equal bar eventually gets the entire plateau. A virtual zero after the final column flushes the positive bars. Repeat for every row and retain the maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This reduces a two-dimensional search to r one-dimensional searches. The heights array is updated in place, but the grid itself is never changed. The sentinel is read as zero rather than appended, so it does not disturb the next row's heights.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grid rows [\"1\", \"0\", \"1\"] and [\"1\", \"1\", \"1\"]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first row heights [1, 0, 1], best area 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second row updates heights to [2, 1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>height 1 pops the first 2, area 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The virtual zero flushes the last 2 and then the 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>height 1 spans all three columns; result \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Inductively each height counts exactly the consecutive ones above and including the current row. In the histogram pass, every popped area is valid because the bars between its boundaries are at least that tall. For any best histogram span, its leftmost minimum bar eventually pops with a width covering that span, including equal-height bars. Thus the histogram maximum is found. Every grid rectangle has a unique bottom row, so taking the maximum over rows covers every candidate and returns the global optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def maximal_rectangle(matrix):\n    if not matrix or not matrix[0]:\n        return 0\n    columns = len(matrix[0])\n    heights = [0] * columns\n    best = 0\n    for row in matrix:\n        for col, cell in enumerate(row):\n            heights[col] = heights[col] + 1 if cell == \"1\" else 0\n        stack = []\n        for right in range(columns + 1):\n            current = heights[right] if right &lt; columns else 0\n            while stack and heights[stack[-1]] &gt; current:\n                height = heights[stack.pop()]\n                left = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n                best = max(best, height * (right - left - 1))\n            if right &lt; columns:\n                stack.append(right)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Updating heights takes O(c) per row. Each column index is pushed and popped at most once in that row's histogram pass, also O(c). Overall time is O(rc), and heights plus the stack take O(c) auxiliary space. The input grid is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reset a column height to zero on a zero cell; a rectangle cannot bridge that cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a fresh stack for each row and flush at its right edge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This interface accepts character cells, not integer 1; mixing representations silently gives the wrong heights.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equal heights are deliberately retained, and the leftmost equal bar supplies their widest candidate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty grids, empty rows, all-zero grids, one-row grids, and one-column grids are covered.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track coordinates of the best rectangle using the popped height, current row, and histogram boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the largest all-zero rectangle by reversing which cell extends a height.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
